.TH PCBVIEW 1
.SH NAME
pcbview \- show the layers of a board
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pcbview
.I layer.vec
\&...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pcbview
shows the
.IR vec (5)
files (from
.IR pcbvec (1),
or from
.IR gbrvec (1)
for the Gerbers of other programs) in a window, the first underneath,
smoothed, in the colors of
KiCad's default theme, on black.
Each file's layer comes from its
.B layer
record, whatever the file's name:
.PP
.RS
.nf
F.Cu                        top copper
In1.Cu ... In4.Cu           inner copper
B.Cu                        bottom copper
F.Silkscreen                silkscreen (B.Silkscreen)
F.Courtyard                 courtyards (B.Courtyard)
F.Mask                      solder mask, at 40% (B.Mask)
F.Paste                     paste, at 90% (B.Paste)
Edge.Cuts                   outline
Drill                       holes; 0.4 mm or less, a via's
.fi
.RE
.PP
A file of no layer known is gray.
The window's title is the absolute path of the directory of the first
file, a slash at the end.
It opens fitted to all the layers loaded, shown or not, as Space fits
them; the wheel zooms round the pointer and the left button drags the
view.
A click within 3 pixels of the box of an element of a part on a layer
shown (a pad, its silk outline, its courtyard, its designator) selects
the part, of the smallest such element under the pointer, and prints
.B select
.I NAME
on stdout; a click on none clears the selection here alone and prints
nothing.
A click on a track, out of the pads, on a layer of copper shown, the
topmost first, selects its connected track as KiCad does: the tracks of
its net joined to it end to end where the end is in no pad (from pad to
pad, or to a via); they are drawn brightened as a selected part, the
net's name stands in a black box in the upper right corner, the part selected is
cleared and nothing is printed; a click on anything else clears it.
The selected part's records are drawn brightened as KiCad draws a
selected item: each color c of brightness br (0.299 r + 0.587 g +
0.117 b) goes toward white by f = 0.25 + br^3, or, if that changes
its brightness by less than 0.05, darkens by 20% with its blue raised
by f.
The keys:
.PP
.RS
.nf
t, b        show or hide the top and bottom copper
1 ... 9     the inner layers by number, on the keypad or not
T, B, S, M, with Shift, any layer key moves its layers on top of
P, Y, E, D  all the others, shown; the others keep their order
! ... (     the same for inner layer 1 to 9 (Shift and its number)
y           the courtyards
s           the silkscreen
m           the solder mask
p           the solder paste
e           the outline
d           the holes
a, A        all the layers on, all off (not z)
z           draw the zones filled or as their outlines (at
            first outlines)
c           center the selected part; again, with nothing
            changed in between, zoom in two times about it
C           zoom out two times about the middle of the window,
            centering nothing
l           show or hide the bar of the layers
Space       fit all
F5          read the files again, the view, the layers shown and
            their order kept; Loading... in the upper right corner
            meanwhile
Esc         deselect the part and the track, here alone; hide
            the help
?           a help of the keys, on and off
q           quit
.fi
.RE
.PP
Zoomed in, the copper shows its names as KiCad does: on each pad its
number over its net, along its longer side, each as large as fits, in
white at 90%; along each track segment long enough its net, 0.7 of its
width high, upright, in white at 70%; each name only once it is at
least 7 pixels high, so a fitted board shows none, and under the copper
of the layers above.
The names are made when the files are read, from the pads and tracks
of the files of copper (a pad's number from its pin record), as text
of a file for each size twice the one before, and a frame draws only
the files large enough.
The masks and paste are always shown.
The outline, the courtyards and the zones' outlines are not things of
the board: they are drawn 1.5 pixels wide at every zoom, and the
outline over all the layers.
A zone is a plane, from a region under a net.
The bar at the top left has the key of each layer loaded, from the top
down, on the layer's color if it is shown, plain if not, and last z, on
gray while the zones are filled (they start as outlines); the key of the layer drawn on top
(Shift) is underlined; a click on a key does as the key.
The pointer over a key names it at once, under the bar ("top copper
(F.Cu)", "inner 1 (In1.Cu)", "silkscreen", "zones: filled", ...).
It reads commands on stdin, a line each:
.B select
.I NAME
selects,
.B select
clears (for scripts: the viewers never send it);
.BR "layer top" ,
.BR "layer bottom" ,
.B layer
.IR N ,
.BR "layer silk" ,
.BR "layer edge" ,
.BR "layer drill" ,
.BR "layer all" ,
.BR "layer none" ,
.BR courtyard ,
.BR center
and
.B bar
as the keys, and
.B zones fill
and
.B zones outline
as z;
.B reload
as F5;
.B shot
.I file
draws the next frame into a PPM picture.
The input of the window is taken before each command, so a picture
shows all the keys and clicks that came before it.
The layers go to the GPU once, as triangles; a frame only sets the
matrix and draws them, by OpenGL, multisampled and in linear light,
the opaque layers from the top down so each pixel is drawn about once.
.SH EXAMPLES
Two viewers cross-probe through two FIFOs:
.PP
.nf
mkfifo a b
schview ch1.vec < a > b &
pcbview bottom.vec in1.vec top.vec drill.vec < b > a &
echo "layer 1" > b
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
schview(1), gbrvec(1), vec(5)
